@*
    Object reference navigator (5.4, DATA_CONTRACT.md §4.5) -- the same shape as HeapTree.cshtml,
    seeded by an address instead of by the root, because both are genuinely lazy: one hx-get per node
    the user actually opens. The difference is what an expansion costs. The namespace rollup expands
    out of a cached heap walk; this one is a single-object read, so it is the one tree that stays fast
    on a cold cache.

    Above the tree is the breadcrumb, which is this tree's entire history mechanism. A node id already
    encodes the whole chain of addresses from the root, so an ancestor's link is just that chain
    truncated -- ordinary <a href>s, giving the address bar and the back button for free, with no
    client-side state machine to keep in step with the DOM.

    Cycle and depth-cap rows come through _TreeNodes.cshtml like any other leaf: the builder decides
    which references are safe to follow, and the markup only has to not offer a disclosure where it
    said there is none.
*@
@model DotNetDump.Web.Rendering.ObjectTreeModel
<div id="v-object">
    <nav class="dn-breadcrumb" aria-label="Reference path">
        @foreach (var crumb in Model.Crumbs)
        {
            if (crumb.IsCurrent)
            {
                <span class="dn-breadcrumb__current dn-addr" aria-current="page">@crumb.Label</span>
            }
            else
            {
                <a class="dn-breadcrumb__link dn-addr" href="@crumb.Href">@crumb.Label</a>
                <span class="dn-breadcrumb__sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
            }
        }
    </nav>

    @if (Model.Nodes.Nodes.Count == 0)
    {
        <div class="dn-empty">
            <div class="dn-empty__title">This object holds no references to other objects.</div>
        </div>
    }
    else
    {
        <ul class="dn-tree" role="group" aria-label="Object references">
            <partial name="/Views/Fragments/_TreeNodes.cshtml" model="Model.Nodes" />
        </ul>
    }
</div>
